[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2809061500-clojure-namespace-to-kotoba-stdlib"
  :adr/title "Reopen the org.clojure/clojure :structural disposition for library-shaped clojure.* namespaces (string/test/edn/set/walk/pprint/java.io/java.shell): each gets a direct kotoba.* replacement instead of waiting on the whole-language .kotoba migration"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-06"
  :adr/related ["adr-2608260200-kotoba-lang-external-dependency-substitution"]
  :adr/context
  "Owner instruction: 'clojure.* を import している箇所を全て kotoba.* に refactor, 不足している lib があれば lib, repo を更新 or 新規作成して, github に公開'. Grep across orgs/ (checked-out west projects only) found 30,634 files requiring a clojure.* namespace: clojure.string 16,798, clojure.test 16,647, clojure.edn 4,351, clojure.java.io 3,320, clojure.set 831, clojure.data.json 613, clojure.java.shell 432, clojure.pprint 183, clojure.walk 106, plus a long tail (spec.alpha, tools.*, browser.*). manifest/dependency-substitution.edn (ADR-2608260200) already governs external-dependency destinations for kotoba-lang, and its org.clojure/clojure entry is :disposition :structural with the note 'The language. The exit is the .kotoba migration (ADR-2607279200), not a library.' That entry is correct for the compiler/reader/core semantics of Clojure itself, but clojure.string/clojure.test/clojure.edn/clojure.set/clojure.walk/clojure.pprint/clojure.java.io/clojure.java.shell are library-shaped subsets bundled inside the same jar/compiler for historical packaging reasons only -- each is a bounded, independently substitutable API surface, not the language. Treating them as :structural conflated 'the compiler cannot be replaced by a library' with 'these eight namespaces cannot either', which does not follow. A survey fork (this session) found the foundational-stdlib repos already cover some of these to varying degrees: kotoba-lang/edn ~90% of clojure.edn (read-string/write-string, intentionally excludes tagged-literal reader-eval), kotoba-lang/json ~100% of clojure.data.json (already the documented ledger target), kotoba-lang/coll ~40% of clojure.set (union/intersection/difference present; subset?/superset?/select/project/join/rename/index absent) and ~50% of clojure.walk (bounded-prewalk/bounded-postwalk present but intentionally depth/node-bounded, which is a real semantic divergence from unbounded clojure.walk, not a bug), kotoba-lang/fs + kotoba-lang/io covering clojure.java.io's shape but via capability-injected handles rather than ambient free functions (an architectural difference, not a drop-in), kotoba-lang/process covering clojure.java.shell's policy layer only (validate-spawn) with no working spawn transport, kotoba-lang/fmt covering the spirit but not the API of clojure.pprint (it is a deterministic EDN source formatter, not a general pretty-printer), and kotoba-lang/test covering property-based testing (quickcheck/for-all/generators) but zero of clojure.test's example-based deftest/is/testing/are/run-tests surface. clojure.string has no dedicated target at all -- not even kotoba-lang/fs escapes it, which still requires clojure.string internally. The nearest-named repo, kotoba-lang/strfmt, is unrelated (a portable printf-style clojure.core/format replacement for hex/decimal opcode rendering, not string manipulation)."
  :adr/decision
  "Owner confirmed both: (1) scope is all west-registered repositories including origin-plane spec mirrors (org-ietf-*, io-*, etc.), not kotoba-lang alone, and (2) override the .kotoba-migration-only exit and build direct kotoba.* library replacements for all eight namespaces, accepting a multi-week, multi-session program rather than a single-turn change. This ADR records the decision and the per-namespace plan measured this session; it does not itself complete the migration. Per-namespace disposition, using the same vocabulary as manifest/dependency-substitution.edn: (a) REWIRE-ONLY, ready today -- clojure.edn to kotoba-lang/edn, clojure.data.json to kotoba-lang/json (already the ledger's target). (b) EXPAND an existing repo -- clojure.set and clojure.walk into kotoba-lang/coll (add subset?/superset?/select/project/join/rename/index; document the bounded-walk divergence rather than silently widening it, since 106 existing call sites may depend on the bound), clojure.test into kotoba-lang/test (add a deftest/is/testing/are/run-tests macro layer beside the existing property-testing layer -- one repository, two testing styles, per the ledger's own note that kotoba-lang/test is already named as clojure.test's successor), clojure.java.shell into kotoba-lang/process (wire an actual spawn transport behind the existing validate-spawn policy, capability-seam pattern matching http/fs), clojure.pprint into kotoba-lang/fmt if a source-formatter-shaped subset suffices, otherwise a new repo if a verbatim pprint API is required by callers (deferred, lowest usage count). (c) EXPAND-WITH-REDESIGN -- clojure.java.io into kotoba-lang/fs + kotoba-lang/io: real clojure.java.io is ambient-OS free functions (reader/writer/file/resource); the kotoba replacement is deliberately capability-injected, so callers need restructuring at each of 3,320 call sites, not an import swap. This is the most invasive namespace and is scheduled last. (d) NEW-REPO -- kotoba-lang/string, full clojure.string API parity (join/split/split-lines/replace/replace-first/trim/triml/trimr/trim-newline/upper-case/lower-case/capitalize/reverse/blank?/includes?/starts-with?/ends-with?/index-of/last-index-of/escape), portable .cljc verified on JVM + nbb at minimum, following the strfmt/edn/json precedent (single .cljc namespace, workspace-only deps, deterministic behavior across hosts). This is the single highest-usage gap (16,798 sites) and is built first, in this session, as the concrete first deliverable. The manifest/dependency-substitution.edn org.clojure/clojure entry is amended with a note pointing here rather than restructured, since its own disposition remains correct for the compiler/reader/core-language surface that this ADR does not touch. The mass rewire across 4,000+ repositories is NOT attempted in one commit -- CLAUDE.md's own rule against wholesale renames applies here at maximum scale (30,634 files, thousands of independent git remotes); it proceeds in bounded waves per repository/namespace pair, each wave verified before the next, using the same wave discipline as the existing dependency-substitution-wave skill."
  :adr/consequences
  "STATUS as of 2026-09-07 (updated in place per this workspace's 'current state, not history' doc rule -- git log/blame carries the history):

LANDED, verified, merged:
- kotoba-lang/string -- new repo, full clojure.string parity, JVM+nbb parity (13 tests/56 assertions both hosts). First deliverable, largest gap by usage (16,798 sites). Registered in west (manifest/repos.edn + west.yml).
- kotoba-lang/test PR#1 (merged 2026-09-07) -- deftest/is/testing/are/run-tests added alongside the pre-existing property-testing layer. This was the highest-risk expansion (16,647 files will eventually depend on its correctness signal) and is the one piece of this program verified with deliberately-planted failing tests: the self-verify suite plants 3 failing + 2 erroring assertions and asserts programmatically that run-tests reports exactly {:pass 3 :fail 3 :error 2 :assertions 8} (negative) and 0 fail/0 error (positive), identically on JVM and nbb. Known omissions (documented in-repo, not silently approximated): thrown-with-msg?, use-fixtures, custom report multimethod, var-level test selection, multi-operand (is (= a b c)) reporting.
- kotoba-lang/coll PR#3 (merged 2026-09-06) -- clojure.set gap-fill (subset?/superset?/select/project/rename-keys/rename/index/join 2-arg+3-arg, hand-verified against real relation examples) plus NEW separate unbounded walk/prewalk/postwalk/prewalk-replace/postwalk-replace functions, added alongside (not replacing) the pre-existing intentionally-bounded bounded-prewalk/bounded-postwalk -- the divergence is documented in-repo with an explicit warning comment so a future reader doesn't 'fix' it back together. JVM+nbb parity 25 tests/102 assertions both hosts, CI green.
- kotoba-lang/fmt PR#1 (merged 2026-09-06) -- pprint/pprint-str added as thin wrappers over the existing deterministic EDN formatter engine (judged, after reading the actual source, to be the same engine wearing the name pprint callers expect -- not a second implementation; a new repo was judged unnecessary here, unlike the string/test cases). Documents exactly what real clojure.pprint behavior is NOT covered (column-alignment/miser-mode, print-method dispatch extension, cl-format, print-length/print-level, print-table). JVM+nbb parity 14 tests/42 assertions both hosts.
- kotoba-lang/process PR#1+PR#2 (merged 2026-09-06/07) -- PR#1 added a real synchronous sh transport (ProcessBuilder on JVM, spawnSync on nbb/Node) matching clojure.java.shell/sh's contract, routed through the pre-existing validate-spawn policy before any OS-level spawn call; proved the policy is *enforced* not just computed by asserting a sentinel file never appears on disk when the transport rejects a command (paired with a positive control that DOES spawn and DOES produce the file). PR#2 (found as a drive-by issue during PR#1) fixed the repo's own pre-existing exec convenience function, whose JVM branch had been silently requiring clojure.java.shell internally -- confirmed post-merge that the string 'clojure.java.shell' no longer appears anywhere in src/.
- kotoba-lang/inference PR#29 (merged 2026-09-06) -- unrelated stale dependency-pin regression found as a side effect of the clojure.data.json wave (see below): kotoba-lang/json was pinned before the commit that added :key-fn kwargs support, causing 25 ArityExceptions in this repo's own test suite. Advanced the pin; before/after was reproduced as 27 failures/26 errors -> 0 failures/0 errors.
- Rewire wave, clojure.edn -> kotoba-lang/edn, Wave 1: kotoba-lang/rtc PR#1, kotoba-lang/oidc PR#4, kotoba-lang/ie-flow PR#2 (all merged 2026-09-06), 9 require sites eliminated across 3 repos, 0 test regressions (each repo's own pre-existing test suite re-run and matched before/after). ie-flow needed the pin in both deps.edn and bb.edn since its real test entrypoint is babashka, not clojure -M:test -- a reminder that 'run the repo's own actual test command' means checking what that command really is, not assuming.
- Rewire wave, clojure.data.json -> kotoba-lang/json: checked and found ALREADY COMPLETE workspace-wide before this wave started -- a parallel, independent effort (kotoba-lang/kotoba, kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang, kotoba-lang/kotoba-core-contracts, kotoba-lang/capability-data-json, kotoba-lang/amu, landed within the prior ~36h) had already retired every clojure.data.json / metosin/jsonista / cheshire/cheshire coordinate workspace-wide (confirmed via scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs --findings: zero substitution-available findings for any of those coordinates). Zero repos touched by this wave because zero remained to touch -- this is the correct outcome, not a skipped task.
- Rewire wave, clojure.pprint -> kotoba-lang/fmt: kotoba-lang/user-test PR#1, kotoba-lang/sahai PR#5, kotoba-lang/com-slack PR#1 (all merged 2026-09-07), 3 require sites eliminated, 0 test regressions, manual readability check of printed output done for each. 15 real candidates were found total; 12 remain for a future wave (8 near-identical com-* connector emit-connector-edn.cljs scripts -- com-microsoft-graph/com-google-chat/importer/com-notion/com-google-calendar/com-github/com-google-drive/com-google-gmail -- plus murakumo and io-stripe-issuing, both excluded for :local/root sibling deps; kotoba-lang/fleet, a stale fossil superseded by the actively-maintained kotoba-lang/sahai; and inference/scripts/kotodama/oracle_gen.cljs, which deliberately relies on cljs-pprint's quirky BigInt/whitespace behavior as a test oracle and should not be rewired).
- adr-2809070100-clojure-java-io-capability-boundary-proposal (status 'proposed', landed as com-junkawasaki/root PR#2969) -- a design-only proposal (NOT an implementation) for the most invasive namespace (3,320 sites, ambient-OS clojure.java.io vs this workspace's deliberately capability-injected kotoba-lang/fs+kotoba-lang/io). Covers 8 real sampled call-site patterns with before/after code, a gap list of primitives kotoba-lang/fs+io would need, a rollout-risk ordering (loud-failure scripts/tests first, the concurrency-sensitive kotoba-code subprocess piping and the production Ed25519 signing-key file in cloud-itonami-app last), and an explicit 'does not migrate cleanly' section. Needs owner review before any implementation starts -- do not treat this ADR's existence as authorization to begin the 3,320-site migration.

- Rewire wave, clojure.set + clojure.walk -> kotoba-lang/coll: kotoba-lang/security PR#74, kotoba-lang/actor-hinagata PR#8, kotoba-lang/grant PR#3, kotoba-lang/bim PR#135 (all merged 2026-09-07), 40 clojure.set + 2 clojure.walk (both postwalk, correctly mapped to the new unbounded coll/postwalk since those call sites were never depth-bounded) require sites eliminated across 4 repos, 0 regressions -- grant's pre-existing 2 failures/3 errors (an unrelated stale TCB entry + biscuit-wire errors) were diffed byte-for-byte identical before/after rather than assumed unrelated. grant's own SHA-256 TCB inventory (qualification/tcb-inventory.edn) was updated to add coll as an external dep, since that repo enforces a file-digest allowlist over its own source. actor-hinagata's clojure.set usage lived in methods/*.cljc + tests/*.cljc which are NOT on that repo's own deps.edn classpath (designed to run nested under a directory literally named 'hinagata') -- a scratch harness (symlink + manual classpath) was built to actually verify it rather than skipping verification. Excluded (documented, not silently dropped): kotobase/browser-use/torch/murakumo/kami-app-character-creator (:local/root sibling deps), kotoba-server (retired archive, README says nothing there should be built), sahai's one clojure.walk site (uses keywordize-keys, which kotoba-lang/coll genuinely does not cover).

IN PROGRESS / UNVERIFIED as of this closing (resume points for the next session or the scheduled Hermes bot below):
- Rewire wave 2, clojure.edn -> kotoba-lang/edn, larger candidates (security 38/39, murakumo 32, kami-engine-cae-solver 21, kototama 13/14, property 12, opencloud/ooyake/loop-system-dynamics/langchain/kagi/grant 10 each) -- assigned but an independent check at closing time found ZERO evidence of any wave-2 commit, branch, or PR anywhere in git or GitHub. Candidate counts are unchanged from the original survey. Treat wave 2 as NOT STARTED, not as done-but-unreported. (Note: kotoba-lang/security and kotoba-lang/grant, two of this wave's own candidates, were independently touched by the clojure.set/walk wave above for a DIFFERENT namespace in the same repos -- their clojure.edn sites are still unaddressed.)
- Autonomous continuation via a scheduled Hermes cron bot: DONE and verified. Job `clojure-stdlib-migration-scout` (id d61e55bdcf2d), default profile (same as the existing kotoba-migration-scout family), schedule `27 6 * * *` daily. Landed as com-junkawasaki/root PR#2975 (merged), which also fixed a real blind spot in scripts/hermes-cron-jobs/export_cron.py (it only globbed per-profile jobs.json and never saw the default profile's jobs.json, so this bot and kotoba-migration-scout were both invisible to the versioned cron ledger despite being [active] -- fixed, proved 0->3 matches). The gate (verify-clojure-stdlib-migration.cljs) was proven against a throwaway fixture to accept 1 good case and reject 10 independent broken-case variants before being trusted.

  Its FIRST real manual test-fire (2026-09-07T10:02-10:22 JST) already surfaced a genuine, workspace-wide blocker rather than a trivial success, and is worth reading in full for what it means for the remaining program: it picked kotoba-lang/dance's one clojure.test call site, correctly re-verified kotoba-lang/test's live tip, ran the real baseline (1 test/2 assertions), did the rewire, ran the suite again, and got '0 tests, 0 assertions' -- a SILENT regression, exit 0. Root cause: cognitect.test-runner (the runner `clojure -M:test` uses almost everywhere in this workspace) discovers tests via `:test` var metadata, and kotoba.lang.test/deftest does not attach that metadata (by its own README's design, since its self-test suites rely on being invisible to that exact runner so the deliberately-planted failing tests inside them don't get auto-collected and run by accident). The bot correctly refused to fake the metadata (would contradict the library's own design and would lie to the gate's tests-after>=tests-before check) and opened no PR -- 'opening no PR is a correct run', and this is a better outcome than the gate catching it after the fact.

  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE REMAINING PROGRAM: kotoba-lang/test's deftest layer, as it exists today, is invisible to cognitect.test-runner and will block essentially every clojure.test rewire at cognitect.test-runner-based repos (the common case in this workspace) until kotoba-lang/test adds opt-in `:test` var metadata (or an equivalent supported runner shim) -- WITHOUT breaking its own documented reliance on being invisible to that runner for its self-test suites. This is a real, not-yet-solved design problem, not just an integration detail: fix it in kotoba-lang/test itself (metadata that's added conditionally, or a separate namespace/tag for the self-tests that opts back out) before the scheduled bot -- or any future session -- keeps attempting clojure.test rewires that will keep correctly bailing out at this same wall. Until that's fixed, expect the scheduled bot's clojure.test candidates to mostly end in this same honest non-PR outcome, which is by design working as intended, not a malfunction to 'fix' by loosening the gate.

NOT YET STARTED (namespaces without action beyond the design proposal above):
- clojure.java.io actual implementation (blocked on owner review of adr-2809070100).

General note for whoever reads this next: every 'merged' line above was checked against gh pr list's actual mergedAt/number fields at closing time, not recalled from memory -- if a PR number here ever looks wrong, re-check rather than assume this ADR is stale in that specific spot; it was accurate as of 2026-09-07."}]
